}

h2 { "Mutation at a glance" }

Reassignment changes a local binding; an element write changes array storage. These are different operations.

table {
  row = ["Operation", "Rule"],
  row = ["Reassign a numeric or Bool scalar", "Allowed locally; the type stays fixed"],
  row = ["Replace a numeric CPU array binding", "Allowed; existing aliases retain the previous array"],
  row = ["Write a numeric or Bool array element", "Requires writable storage and no unfinished task readers"],
  row = ["Write through an array parameter", "The parameter must declare mut"],
  row = ["Edit a String or tensor in place", "Not supported; construct a new value"],
  row = ["Update a record", "Use with to construct a new record"]
}

list {
  link { url = "/docs/arrays", "Array access, copying, sorting and shared readers" },
  link { url = "/docs/functions#array-access-contracts", "Function parameter access contracts" }
}

h2 { "Functions and control flow" }

Functions return their final expression. A function value can be passed to another function or retain values from its defining scope as a closure. Conditions choose which branch runs, while repeats a body, and match selects a declared enum or error alternative. Function signatures record parameter access and any expected-error channel.

list {
  link { url = "/docs/functions", "Definitions, function values, closures and recursion" },
  link { url = "/docs/control-flow", "Conditions and pattern matching" },
  link { url = "/docs/errors", "Error declarations, try, fail and catch" },
  link { url = "/docs/modules", "Imports, exports and re-exports" }
}

h2 { "Tasks and placement" }

spawn, await, parallel, try and on are language constructs, not ordinary functions. A parallel scope owns its children and settles them before leaving. Device selectors and transfer are documented separately as callable built-ins. A tensor result awaited from a GPU task remains resident until an explicit transfer changes its placement.

list {
  link { url = "/docs/parallelism", "Structured CPU tasks" },
  link { url = "/docs/gpu", "GPU placement" },
  link { url = "/docs/task-functions", "Task and device built-ins" }
}

h2 { "Operators and evaluation" }

Numeric expressions use +, -, *, / and integer remainder %. String concatenation uses +. Comparison operators are ==, !=, <, <=, > and >=; their accepted operands must have compatible types. Bool expressions use not, and and or. Conditions and Boolean and/or use lazy selection, so an unselected expression does not run. Comparison chaining requires explicit grouping rather than Python-style chained comparisons.

Multiplication, division and remainder bind more tightly than addition and subtraction. The -> pipeline passes its left expression as the first argument of the call on its right. Use parentheses to make the intended grouping explicit, especially when combining a pipeline with arithmetic or comparisons.

Indexes are zero-based. Array and String indexing uses one index; tensor indexing uses one per dimension. Bounds are checked. An indexed expression reads a value. Numeric and Bool CPU arrays also permit checked element assignment when the caller has write access and no unfinished task readers share that storage. Strings and tensors do not support in-place edits.
